1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,420
You know, when we look at government infrastructure, it's, uh,

2
00:00:03,080 --> 00:00:06,000
it's pretty easy to just picture a ribbon-cutting ceremony.

3
00:00:06,040 --> 00:00:07,540
Yeah, like building a physical bridge.

4
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:11,800
Exactly. You know, the bridge is built, the photo is taken, and everyone just goes home.

5
00:00:12,000 --> 00:00:14,500
Right. Because the physical structure has a clear finish line.

6
00:00:15,060 --> 00:00:22,000
But, um, step into the world of digital architecture for government systems, and that finish line completely vanishes.

7
00:00:22,360 --> 00:00:29,600
Which brings us to today's deep dive. We're looking at phase five of digital architecture, which is called Execute and Govern.

8
00:00:30,140 --> 00:00:40,020
Yes. And we'll be using our demonstration country, Progressa, pronounced progress, to see how a theoretical roadmap actually survives the real world.

9
00:00:40,040 --> 00:00:45,380
Right. And if you're a government official managing these systems in your ministry or, you know, your program,

10
00:00:46,080 --> 00:00:51,340
this is exactly how you prevent your digital systems from becoming completely fragmented and duplicated.

11
00:00:51,460 --> 00:00:52,180
It really is.

12
00:00:52,260 --> 00:00:56,660
And here is the truly fascinating part: phase five is actually the phase that never ends.

13
00:00:57,120 --> 00:01:04,860
Yeah. It's all about turning an approved national enterprise architecture roadmap into, well, a continuous project pipeline.

14
00:01:05,200 --> 00:01:07,460
So how does Progressa actually pull that off?

15
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:12,280
Well, it relies on three main things. First, the repository goes live.

16
00:01:12,380 --> 00:01:14,860
Okay. As in, like the single store of architecture.

17
00:01:15,020 --> 00:01:16,440
Right. It holds the layers,

18
00:01:17,060 --> 00:01:22,200
uh, the sourcing decisions, the principles, and crucially, the decision log.

19
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:26,290
Oh, wow. So if someone tries to override a standard three years from now-

20
00:01:26,420 --> 00:01:29,500
You have the exact record of why that standard was set in the first place.

21
00:01:29,560 --> 00:01:30,870
That is incredibly useful.

22
00:01:30,900 --> 00:01:33,560
It is, and it's managed by one named architect.

23
00:01:34,120 --> 00:01:38,080
But, you know, a repository is just a dead database if no one is constantly updating it.

24
00:01:38,200 --> 00:01:40,670
Right. Which brings us to the second thing, right?

25
00:01:40,700 --> 00:01:41,540
The permanent team.

26
00:01:41,620 --> 00:01:47,820
Exactly. A team of two to four architects who act as the standing muscle for cross-cutting decisions.

27
00:01:48,080 --> 00:01:49,560
Okay, wait. Let me push back on that for a second.

28
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:50,060
Sure. Go ahead.

29
00:01:50,480 --> 00:01:54,700
Why fund a permanent team if there are no active projects running at a given moment?

30
00:01:55,280 --> 00:01:58,620
I mean, it sounds like paying a construction crew to just stand around in an empty lot.

31
00:01:58,920 --> 00:02:04,860
I get that. But remember, this team reports directly to the digitalization officer.

32
00:02:04,870 --> 00:02:11,930
Maintaining the P-EAR, uh, uh, the Public Administration Ecosystem Reference Architecture is an ongoing practice.

33
00:02:12,180 --> 00:02:14,460
Oh, because external technology keeps evolving.

34
00:02:14,520 --> 00:02:18,020
Exactly. Security protocols change, legacy systems decay.

35
00:02:18,080 --> 00:02:21,540
If you don't have that team, cross-system alignment just disappears entirely.

36
00:02:21,600 --> 00:02:25,540
Okay, that makes sense. So what is the third thing making phase five real?

37
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:31,850
The review gate. Let's say a new well-funded scholarship program arrives in Progressa in a real hurry.

38
00:02:32,090 --> 00:02:34,160
Which, uh, happens all the time in government.

39
00:02:34,240 --> 00:02:35,200
Oh, definitely. Yeah.

40
00:02:35,220 --> 00:02:40,780
And they plan to build their own small learner register because integrating with the existing one just seems too slow.

41
00:02:40,840 --> 00:02:47,460
I mean, that is like a department buying entirely new software just to avoid learning the secure centralized system everyone else uses.

42
00:02:47,560 --> 00:02:48,230
Exactly. Wow.

43
00:02:48,240 --> 00:02:51,520
It's a totally rational, fast choice for that specific project.

44
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,340
But incredibly costly for the whole of government.

45
00:02:53,540 --> 00:03:00,420
Yes. And this is where the review gate steps in to ask a very simple question: Does a shared building block already exist?

46
00:03:00,700 --> 00:03:02,980
And in this case, the answer is yes.

47
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:05,240
The learner register from wave one.

48
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:07,250
Exactly. They don't need a new one.

49
00:03:07,280 --> 00:03:08,700
So what does the board actually do then?

50
00:03:08,720 --> 00:03:09,940
Do they step in?

51
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:10,220
Yes.

52
00:03:10,880 --> 00:03:16,400
The board rules that the project absolutely must consume the existing learner register.

53
00:03:16,440 --> 00:03:18,359
They cannot build a fourth list.

54
00:03:18,400 --> 00:03:21,220
But couldn't a simple procurement rule catch that?

55
00:03:21,240 --> 00:03:22,740
Like, you know, when checking budgets.

56
00:03:23,340 --> 00:03:25,630
No, a standard procurement rule wouldn't catch this at all.

57
00:03:25,780 --> 00:03:32,460
That only looks at vendors. To catch this requires a whole of government view and a board that can actually say no.

58
00:03:32,840 --> 00:03:34,440
What happens if the board doesn't step in?

59
00:03:34,640 --> 00:03:35,520
Let's say they just, you know,

60
00:03:36,200 --> 00:03:36,900
let it slide.

61
00:03:37,040 --> 00:03:41,720
Well, if they let it slide, the systems become highly fragmented, siloed, and duplicated.

62
00:03:41,800 --> 00:03:43,260
Just redundant data everywhere.

63
00:03:43,300 --> 00:03:47,380
Right. The same citizen data gets rekeyed across multiple systems.

64
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:51,540
You end up locked into one supplier, and it becomes incredibly costly to change.

65
00:03:52,040 --> 00:03:54,520
Wow. So how do you prevent that in the long term?

66
00:03:54,560 --> 00:04:01,960
To prevent it, the board sits the business side and the IT side over the same repository four times a year, so they decide together forever.

67
00:04:02,020 --> 00:04:04,980
Every single quarter. That is a serious commitment.

68
00:04:05,060 --> 00:04:09,820
It is, but that's how you establish the standing muscle to keep things permanently aligned.

69
00:04:09,840 --> 00:04:11,260
It goes back to what we said at the start.

70
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:15,180
Digital architecture isn't a physical bridge where you just cut a ribbon and walk away.

71
00:04:15,340 --> 00:04:25,220
No, it's a living practice. You need to keep the repository live, use that review gate to catch duplicated efforts early, and make sure the board's decisions

72
00:04:25,240 --> 00:04:25,850
are binding.

73
00:04:26,360 --> 00:04:29,700
Which is a huge takeaway for anyone listening who works in a ministry.

74
00:04:29,740 --> 00:04:31,520
It's all about that continuous oversight.

